<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Sacred War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 595–<a href="/years/585-bce">585 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Sacred War was fought in the early 6th century BCE by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delphic-amphictyony">Amphictyonic League</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, Sicyon under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, against the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirrha">Kirrha</a>, accused of abusing pilgrims to Delphi. Kirrha was destroyed and its plain dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>. Afterwards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythian-games">Pythian Games</a> were reorganised and Delphi came under the Amphictyony's protection.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war, also called the Cirraean War, was fought between the Pylaeo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delphic-amphictyony">Delphic Amphictyony</a> and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirrha">Kirrha</a>. The Amphictyony, controlled at the time by the Thessalians, sought to take hold of the Sacred Land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, the Kirrhaean Plain. Kirrha was charged with frequent robbery and mistreatment of pilgrims on their way to Delphi and with encroaching on Delphic land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes-of-sicyon">Cleisthenes of Sicyon</a> led the attack, using his navy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> the city's port before an allied Amphictyonic army laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirrha">Kirrha</a>. An Athenian contingent served under Alcmaeon, and the Thessalian leaders were Eurylochos and Hippias. The siege is remembered for an early use of poison in war. The earliest and probably most reliable account, by the 5th-century BCE medical writer Thessalos, says that the attackers found a secret water pipe into the city after a horse's hoof broke it, and that an asclepiad named Nebros advised them to poison the water with hellebore, which left the defenders too weak with diarrhoea to resist.</p><p>Later writers told it differently. Frontinus, in the 1st century CE, said the pipe was first cut and then restored with poisoned water once the city was desperate with thirst. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyaenus">Polyaenus</a>, in the 2nd century, had the hellebore, gathered at Anticyra, put into the spring and credited the plan to Eurylochus. Pausanias wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> diverted the River Pleistos and then poisoned its water as it flowed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirrha">Kirrha</a>. Because Nebros was held to be an ancestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a>, some have wondered whether guilt over the poisoning lay behind the Hippocratic Oath.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirrha">Kirrha</a> were dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leto">Leto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>, and it was forbidden to cultivate them or graze animals on them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> received a third of the booty and played a leading part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythian-games">Pythian Games</a> held to celebrate the end of the fighting. Modern scholars are very sceptical about the exact events and about the war's long duration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Sacred War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Delphians had little farmland of their own, and the plain below the city, consecrated to Apollo after the <b>First Sacred War</b>, was by law left uncultivated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its role became prominent in the <b>First Sacred War</b>, traditionally c. 595-585 BCE, when members destroyed the town of Kirrha for abusing pilgrims and then reorganised the Pythian Games in 582 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/amphictyonic-league">Amphictyonic League</a></li><li><blockquote>The tyrant Cleisthenes, early 6th century BCE, renamed the Dorian tribes, fought Argos and helped win the <b>First Sacred War</b>; his daughter Agariste married into the Athenian Alcmaeonids.</blockquote><a href="/state/sicyon">Sicyon</a></li></ul></section>
